the eye-watering spike in the cost of  gas is painful for consumers and  businesses this is the price of  wholesale gas and it's up more than 500  percent since the start of the year all  countries are facing the same  stratospheric surge in prices but when  it comes to generating electricity some  are better insulated against the impact  let's look at the uk's energy mix at  midday on monday gas made up almost 50  percent renewables around a quarter  nuclear a little over 10  and there's a little bit there from coal  too  compare that to last october and you can  see renewables are roughly the same but  we're using a lot more gas now and  that's to make up for a sharp drop in  nuclear generation as half a dozen  reactors have been shut down for  maintenance  it's a very different situation in  france there almost 80 percent of  electricity is generated with nuclear  energy gas makes up less than 10  significantly less than the uk so the  direct impact on electricity consumers  is much lower  france also generates enough energy to  export to other countries  germany also has a very different energy  mix to the uk just 13 of its electricity  was generated with gas at midday  but look at coal it made up almost a  third but germany's heavy use of coal  comes with a climate cost the dirtiest  of all the fossil fuels produces the  equivalent of around 800 grams of carbon  dioxide per kilowatt of energy that's  twice as much as gas for comparison  offshore wind is just 10 grams per  kilowatt  coal just does not fit inside the carbon  budgets that we have and it doesn't fit  inside the international commitments  that we're signing up to  and on the economics point  it's cheaper than gas at the minute but  the lesson from the gas crisis should be  that fossil fuel prices are very fickle  very volatile  and coal could just as easily become  expensive another time so rather than  trying to  pick the least worst fossil fuel and  hoping it stays cheap  you should be trying to move away from  fossil fuels entirely back to  homegrown renewables  the energy crunch is worldwide india's  135 coal power stations which generate  70 percent of the country's electricity  have their own supply crisis more than  half have stocks to last just three days  with blackouts a growing reality as  economies are restarting after the  pandemic energy is in high demand
 

